@id     01_hello
@title  Hello, C
@topic  output
@lang   c
@show   values

@theory
Every C program starts running in main(). To print text, use printf from <stdio.h>.

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void) {
        printf("Hi!\n");
        return 0;
    }

"\n" moves to a new line. return 0 tells the system the program finished without errors.

Your task:
Make the program print exactly these two lines:

    Hello, CodeLens!
    I am learning C.

- Write your code under the comment "print the two lines here".
- Use one printf for each line.

**Hint:** end each line with `\n`, like `printf("Hi!\n");`

@read
## What is a program?
A program is a list of instructions. The computer runs them one by one, from top to bottom. In C, every program starts running at the first line inside main.

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void) {
        printf("Hello!\n");
        return 0;
    }

## The parts of this program
- `#include <stdio.h>` lets the program use printf. stdio means "standard input and output".
- `int main(void) { ... }` is where the program starts. Everything between { and } belongs to main.
- `printf("Hello!\n");` prints the text between the quotes on the screen.
- `return 0;` ends the program and tells the computer "everything went well".

## Small details that matter
- Every instruction ends with a semicolon `;`. Forgetting it is the most common beginner error.
- `\n` is a "new line". Without it, the next text continues on the same line.
- Text in quotes is printed exactly as written, including spaces.

    printf("Hi ");
    printf("there!\n");     // prints: Hi there!

## Read more
Beej's Guide to C Programming, chapter 2 "Hello, World!" — free at beej.us/guide/bgc

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    // print the two lines here

    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Hello, CodeLens!\n");
    printf("I am learning C.\n");
    return 0;
}

@test
@expected
Hello, CodeLens!
I am learning C.

#! ------------------------------------------------------------------
#! Steps: Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step watch
@title  Your first program
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Hello!\n");
    printf("I am a program.\n");
    return 0;
}
@text
Press Next line and watch what the program prints.
A program is a list of instructions that run from top to bottom, starting inside main.
printf prints the text between the quotes, and \n starts a new line.

@step choose
@title  Find the printing line
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Hello!\n");
    return 0;
}
@text
Which line prints text on the screen?
@option #include <stdio.h>
@option int main(void) {
@option printf("Hello!\n");
@option return 0;
@correct 3
@answer
printf prints the text in quotes. #include brings printf into the program, main is where
the program starts, and return 0 ends it.

@step predict
@title  Predict the output
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Hi ");
    printf("there!\n");
    return 0;
}
@text
What does this program print?
Look closely: the first printf has no \n, so the next text continues on the same line.

@step order
@title  Build a program
@text
Put the lines in the right order to make a program that prints Hello, world!
Every program starts with #include, then main with its instructions inside { }.
@code
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    return 0;
}

@step modify
@title  Change the message
@text
Change the program so it prints Hello, CodeLens! instead of Hello, world!
Only the printf line can be edited. Press Run to try it, then Check.
@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    return 0;
}
@editable 4-4
@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Hello, CodeLens!\n");
    return 0;
}
@test
@expected
Hello, CodeLens!

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
In your own words: what does printf do, and what is \n for?
@rubric
prints; text; screen; new line
@answer
printf prints the text between the quotes on the screen. \n moves to a new line, so the
next text starts on the line below.

